Q3)
i) MEMORY ISSUE:
The issue is poor memory locality rather than insufficient RAM.Large heap accessed randomly
REASONS:
Threads access scattered memory pages
Working set exceeds cache/TLB efficiency
Frequent page replacement
ii) OPTIMIZATIONS:
1) Reduce Heap Usage
Free unused memory
Use memory pools
Allocate memory efficiently
This reduces page faults
2) Reduce Stack Size / Number of Threads
Use thread pools
Reduce stack size if possible
Reuse threads
This lowers memory overhead

Q4)
i) ROOT CAUSE:
The scheduler always executes higher-priority queues first
As new processes continuously enter Q1 and Q2,
 Q1 → Executed
 Q2 → Executed
 Q1 → Executed
 Q2 → Executed
 Q3 is never selected
 P5 suffers starvation
ii) OPTIMIZATION:
Aging
Increase the priority of processes waiting for a long time
Eventually P5 reaches a higher-priority queue and gets CPU time
Periodically allocate CPU time to Q3
Use Multilevel Feedback Queue (MLFQ)

